<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  One line of widget text in Ubuntu Sans bold.

  Glance cannot carry a custom font: its TextStyle.fontFamily becomes a TypefaceSpan, and
  TypefaceSpan parcels only the family *name*, which the launcher resolves against system
  fonts — an app font is not registered there, so it silently falls back. A layout inflated
  through RemoteViews is different: the launcher inflates it against this package's own
  resources, as it does the meteocon flipper, so @font here resolves to the bundled face.

  One layout per weight because TextView.setTypeface is not a remotable method, so weight
  cannot be applied at runtime the way text, size and colour can.

  match_parent width, so the line wraps inside the column it is placed in. As wrap_content
  the TextView measured itself against the whole unwrapped string and ran off the edge of
  the card, taking the rest of the layout with it.
-->
<!--
  fallbackLineSpacing is off deliberately.

  Hadith text contains the ligature U+FDFA (ﷺ), which Ubuntu Sans has no glyph for, so it
  is drawn from a system fallback font with much taller metrics. With the default (true),
  TextView grows that whole line box to fit the fallback, and the paragraph ends up with
  visibly uneven leading — lines carrying the ligature spaced far apart, the runs between
  them cramped. Off, every line uses the primary font's metrics and the block reads evenly.

  Ignored below API 28, which simply keeps the old behaviour there.
-->
<TextView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/widget_text"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:fontFamily="@font/ubuntu_sans_bold"
    android:textStyle="bold"
    android:includeFontPadding="false"
    android:fallbackLineSpacing="false"
    android:ellipsize="end" />
